LINUX BASICS - SESSION 3

USER AND GROUP MANAGEMENT

User management is important for accountability and security. Each person should use a separate account instead of sharing the root account.

The root user is the system administrator. Root can read, change, or delete almost anything, so use it carefully.

1. IMPORTANT SYSTEM FILES

/etc contains system configuration files.

/etc/passwd contains basic information about local users, such as the username, user ID, group ID, home directory, and login shell.

/etc/shadow stores password hashes and password-aging information. It should only be readable by privileged users.

/etc/group contains local group information.

View these files with:

cat /etc/passwd
cat /etc/group

Do not edit these files manually during normal administration. Use commands such as useradd, usermod, groupadd, and passwd.

2. CREATING USERS

useradd devops
Creates a user account. On some distributions, this command does not create the home directory unless an option is supplied.

Create a user and its home directory:

sudo useradd -m devops

Set or change the user's password:

sudo passwd devops

The password is not displayed while it is being typed.

On Debian and Ubuntu, adduser is an interactive command that creates the account, home directory, and user information:

sudo adduser devops

useradd is commonly used in scripts because it is non-interactive. adduser is convenient for creating users manually, but it asks questions during the process.

Check the account:

id devops
getent passwd devops

3. PASSWORDS AND PASSWORD AGING

Linux stores a one-way password hash, not the original password. A normal user cannot decrypt the hash to recover a forgotten password.

If an administrator has appropriate privileges, they can reset the password without recovering the old one:

sudo passwd devops

Require the user to choose a new password at the next login:

sudo chage -d 0 devops

Set the maximum password age to 90 days:

sudo chage -M 90 devops

View password-aging information:

sudo chage -l devops

Password policies should follow the organization's security requirements. A password expiration period by itself is not a complete security policy.

4. LOCKING AND UNLOCKING ACCOUNTS

Lock an account without deleting it:

sudo passwd -l devops

Unlock the account:

sudo passwd -u devops

Locking is useful when access must be suspended temporarily or while an account is being investigated. Confirm the organization's policy before unlocking an account.

5. DELETING USERS

When an employee leaves an organization, first confirm ownership of files, running processes, scheduled jobs, and service accounts. Then disable or remove access according to the organization's offboarding process.

Delete the user but keep the home directory:

sudo userdel devops

Delete the user and its home directory:

sudo userdel -r devops

The -r option is destructive. Back up or transfer any required files before using it.

Confirm that the account is gone:

getent passwd devops

No output normally means that the account was not found.

6. CREATING AND USING GROUPS

Groups make permission management easier. Instead of changing permissions for 250 developers individually, place them in a group and assign permissions to that group.

Create a group:

sudo groupadd devops_training

Add an existing user to the group:

sudo usermod -aG devops_training devops

The -a option appends the group. Without -a, the user's existing supplementary groups may be replaced.

Check the user's groups:

id devops
groups devops

Check the group entry:

getent group devops_training

The user may need to log out and log in again before a new group membership appears in the session. A user can also start a new login shell with:

su - devops

Only use su when you have permission to access the target account. The command may ask for the target user's password.

7. FILE PERMISSIONS AND GROUPS

When a directory or file is owned by a group, permissions assigned to that group apply to all users in the group.

View ownership and permissions:

ls -ld /path/to/directory

Example group ownership and permissions:

sudo chgrp devops_training /shared/project
sudo chmod 2770 /shared/project

The 2 in 2770 enables the setgid bit on the directory, so new files normally inherit the directory's group. Test permission changes in a safe practice directory before applying them to a production system.

8. ROOT AND DANGEROUS COMMANDS

sudo su -
Starts a root login shell when the current user is allowed to use sudo. Use sudo for individual commands when possible, because a root shell makes accidental damage easier.

Never run this as a practice exercise:

rm -rf /sbin

/sbin contains essential system programs on many Linux systems. Removing it can make the system unusable. In modern distributions, /sbin may also be a symbolic link into /usr/sbin. Always inspect a path carefully before using rm -r or rm -rf.

9. CONNECTING TO A REMOTE LINUX SERVER WITH SSH

An SSH server usually runs the sshd service on the remote Linux machine. An SSH client runs on your laptop or workstation. Git Bash on Windows can provide the ssh client.

Connect with:

ssh username@public-ip-address

The first connection may ask you to verify the server's host key. Verify it through a trusted source before accepting it. Then enter the account password or use the configured SSH key.

After connecting, identify the system:

whoami
uname -a
pwd

PRACTICE

1. Create a test user with a home directory.

	sudo useradd -m trainee
	sudo passwd trainee
	id trainee

2. Create a group and add the test user.

	sudo groupadd training
	sudo usermod -aG training trainee
	id trainee

3. Require a password change at the next login and inspect the policy.

	sudo chage -d 0 trainee
	sudo chage -l trainee

4. Lock and unlock the test account.

	sudo passwd -l trainee
	sudo passwd -u trainee

5. Remove the test user and its home directory after confirming that it is only a practice account.

	sudo userdel -r trainee

QUICK RECAP

Users provide identity and accountability.
Passwords are stored as one-way hashes; forgotten passwords are reset, not decrypted.
Use chage for password-aging policies.
Lock accounts with passwd -l and unlock them with passwd -u.
Groups allow permissions to be managed for many users at once.
Use useradd in scripts and adduser for interactive account creation on Debian-based systems.
Use root and rm -rf with extreme care.
Use SSH to connect securely to a remote Linux server.
